{"id": "wd-Q10901792", "status": "ok", "meta": "King Midas begs Bacchus to take back his golden touch in a painting usually attributed to Nicolas Poussin, now in Munich.", "about": "King Midas kneels in a blue robe and yellow cloak and begs Bacchus to take back his gift of turning everything he touches to gold. Bacchus stands beside him with a cup and holds out a hand to the suffering king. A naked nymph sleeps in the foreground with an infant, and fauns drink, play pipes and pick fruit behind. Anthony Blunt doubted the attribution to Nicolas Poussin in 1966, but the Alte Pinakothek accepts it as an early work.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AMidas_und_Bacchus%2C_um_1624.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AMidas_und_Bacchus%2C_um_1624.jpg", "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q10901792", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midas_and_Bacchus_(Poussin)"], "check": "clean"}
